 {:db/id "q-108371" :question/id #uuid "0da8771f-0983-5e77-be97-afe167de38df" :question/math-academy-id "q-108371" :question/problem "Find the intersection point of the line $l$ and the plane $Π$ with equations\n\n$$\nl:r=⟨4,-2,3⟩+w⟨5,-1,2⟩,\\,Π:r=⟨10,-5,12⟩+s⟨4,3,0⟩+t⟨0,2,5⟩.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "From the equation of a plane in the parametric form $r=p+tv+sw$, we have that its normal vector is $n=v\\times w.$ In our case, the normal vector to $Π$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =v\\times w \\\\  & =⟨4,3,0⟩\\times ⟨0,2,5⟩ \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 4 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 5\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 5\\end{aligned}|i-|\\begin{aligned}4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 5\\end{aligned}|j+|\\begin{aligned}4 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}|k \\\\  & =15i-20j+8k \\\\  & =⟨15,-20,8⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the plane $Π$ in dot product form is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r\\cdot n & =p\\cdot n \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨15,-20,8⟩ & =⟨10,-5,12⟩\\cdot ⟨15,-20,8⟩ \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨15,-20,8⟩ & =346.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, substituting $r=⟨4,-2,3⟩+w⟨5,-1,2⟩$ into the equation of the plane in dot product form, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r\\cdot ⟨15,-20,8⟩ & =346 \\\\ ⟨4+5w,-2-w,3+2w⟩\\cdot ⟨15,-20,8⟩ & =346 \\\\ 15(4+5w)-20(-2-w)+8(3+2w) & =346 \\\\ 60+75w+40+20w+24+16w & =346 \\\\ 111w & =222 \\\\ w & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, to get the position vector $q$ of the intersection point $Q,$ we substitute $w=2$ into the equation of the line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}q & =⟨4+5(2),-2-(2),3+2(2)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨14,-4,7⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the intersection point is $Q(14,-4,7).$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["q-108371/ma-source-field/5f43a1b61d82a94090cbfa2ed6efc4e8ae17db6f6c1ef72c16e10134342bf269"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]}
