 {:db/id "q-109307/ma-source-field/6419da1a93d5cd0ca97f642fd7f318b071afcdc22407326bcdd3fc83fcc4ef6c" :answer-field/id #uuid "1669c4fe-448a-5ecd-96c2-60941d083824" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/choices ["q-109307/ma-source-field/6419da1a93d5cd0ca97f642fd7f318b071afcdc22407326bcdd3fc83fcc4ef6c/337871c4ded8b56d27da847dac48b5d852318639dc7a5676356beeacb3a5d3e8" "q-109307/ma-source-field/6419da1a93d5cd0ca97f642fd7f318b071afcdc22407326bcdd3fc83fcc4ef6c/46696b5f18bbc7afdc9b9f3cdb86f29fd173bd0b0400cd1896469fb5bb02d18e" "q-109307/ma-source-field/6419da1a93d5cd0ca97f642fd7f318b071afcdc22407326bcdd3fc83fcc4ef6c/0b12335919e50b6698ef6f571d6783502885f1498b10edff7f902d79221db5e7" "q-109307/ma-source-field/6419da1a93d5cd0ca97f642fd7f318b071afcdc22407326bcdd3fc83fcc4ef6c/e3427781e70a2d1b808876cce6c526bd7be48594bd52dad92362fe49805142ad" "q-109307/ma-source-field/6419da1a93d5cd0ca97f642fd7f318b071afcdc22407326bcdd3fc83fcc4ef6c/345365c355de120a46d8a696fb26df5506e0a31c24cbb1be4abe3740ccc1a9f6"] :answer-field/correct "q-109307/ma-source-field/6419da1a93d5cd0ca97f642fd7f318b071afcdc22407326bcdd3fc83fcc4ef6c/345365c355de120a46d8a696fb26df5506e0a31c24cbb1be4abe3740ccc1a9f6" :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]}
 {:db/id "q-109307" :question/id #uuid "7704a427-55d1-5f8e-bf57-d941d8544127" :question/math-academy-id "q-109307" :question/problem "A particle $P$ is traveling along the $x$-axis with displacement $s(t)$ from a fixed origin $O$ given by\n\n$$\ns(t)=7{t}^{2}-{t}^{3},\\,t\\ge 0.\n$$\n\nFind the time at which the acceleration is zero, and find the value of the velocity at this moment." :question/worked-solution "First, we calculate $v(t)$ by differentiating $s(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}s}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(7{t}^{2}-{t}^{3}) \\\\  & =14t-3{t}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we differentiate $v(t)$ to find $a(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(14t-3{t}^{2}) \\\\  & =14-6t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we put $a=0$ to find the time when the acceleration is zero:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}14-6t & =0 \\\\ -6t & =-14 \\\\ t & =\\frac{7}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the acceleration is zero when $t=\\frac{7}{3}.$ To find the velocity at this moment, we substitute this back into $v(t)$ to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(\\frac{7}{3}) & =14(\\frac{7}{3})-3{(\\frac{7}{3})}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{98}{3}-\\frac{49}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{49}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-109307/ma-source-field/6419da1a93d5cd0ca97f642fd7f318b071afcdc22407326bcdd3fc83fcc4ef6c"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]}
