 {:db/id "q-109639/ma-source-field/3663e9fb1b3f4378a29c1d1ba80d6a8c7344edaf834f63d6aa008e6a975f0c19" :answer-field/id #uuid "8605a456-8f74-549b-ba68-da660540b8c0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/choices ["q-109639/ma-source-field/3663e9fb1b3f4378a29c1d1ba80d6a8c7344edaf834f63d6aa008e6a975f0c19/351b304967dcc6cc71954838aff06c1bdec7c141ca97f1835a7fb4804d2dd1b5" "q-109639/ma-source-field/3663e9fb1b3f4378a29c1d1ba80d6a8c7344edaf834f63d6aa008e6a975f0c19/af9b77265c9768ef55dc86016fdbdbc8bf7bb53ef087c2604a4dc12c8db42a8e" "q-109639/ma-source-field/3663e9fb1b3f4378a29c1d1ba80d6a8c7344edaf834f63d6aa008e6a975f0c19/4c2f4ae4766e562e23988c2253523d50b1a061544b651a17c545fc5be42466e4" "q-109639/ma-source-field/3663e9fb1b3f4378a29c1d1ba80d6a8c7344edaf834f63d6aa008e6a975f0c19/38bf24cb1ff7587d93e62c4a9fc1fdbaca3311d2866ab6cf85dec7a4f74cf9da" "q-109639/ma-source-field/3663e9fb1b3f4378a29c1d1ba80d6a8c7344edaf834f63d6aa008e6a975f0c19/c5512c2a6abd89eb4c1b600e394b59593c2497fb05a04fdb668724db43a68145"] :answer-field/correct "q-109639/ma-source-field/3663e9fb1b3f4378a29c1d1ba80d6a8c7344edaf834f63d6aa008e6a975f0c19/351b304967dcc6cc71954838aff06c1bdec7c141ca97f1835a7fb4804d2dd1b5" :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]}
 {:db/id "q-109639" :question/id #uuid "ebf9231a-650d-5b8c-8818-efc1d0c42664" :question/math-academy-id "q-109639" :question/problem "The second-order initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}-4{e}^{t}{y}^{′}+5y=0,\\,y(0)=2,\\,{y}^{′}(0)=-1\n$$\n\ncan be written as a system of first-order differential equations, as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}={x}_{2},\\, & {x}_{1}(0)=p \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}=f(t),\\, & {x}_{2}(0)=q\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{1}(t)=y(t),$ ${x}_{2}(t)={y}^{′}(t),$ and $p$ and $q$ are constants. Calculate  $(p-q)\\cdot f(t).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we make the following change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}(t) & =y \\\\ {x}_{2}(t) & ={y}^{′}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe also convert the initial conditions, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(0) & ={x}_{1}(0)=2 \\\\ {y}^{′}(0) & ={x}_{2}(0)=-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDifferentiating the above equations with respect to $t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t) & ={y}^{′} \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t) & ={y}^{″}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich we can rewrite as \n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}={x}_{2}, \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}={y}^{″}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAt this point, we're almost done. We just need to write the right-hand side of the last equation in terms of ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}.$\n\nSolving our original differential equation for the highest-order derivative $({y}^{″}),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}=4{e}^{t}{y}^{′}-5y.\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute our new variables into the right-hand side of the above equation. This gives\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}=4{e}^{t}{x}_{2}-5{x}_{1}.\n$$\n\nNotice that we do not include any derivative terms on the right-hand side. \n\nTherefore, our complete system of equations is as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}={x}_{2},\\, & {x}_{1}(0)=2 \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}=-5{x}_{1}+4{e}^{t}{x}_{2},\\, & {x}_{2}(0)=-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, $p=2,q=-1,f(t)=-5{x}_{1}+4{x}_{2},$ and \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(p-q)\\cdot f(t) & =(2-(-1))(-5{x}_{1}+4{e}^{t}{x}_{2}) \\\\  & =3(-5{x}_{1}+4{e}^{t}{x}_{2}) \\\\  & =-15{x}_{1}+12{e}^{t}{x}_{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-109639/ma-source-field/3663e9fb1b3f4378a29c1d1ba80d6a8c7344edaf834f63d6aa008e6a975f0c19"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]}
