 {:db/id "q-110569/ma-source-field/4ad9718c1de7d4799fb874170518641b6a8b41e3a629d747aaebbf6123a3ea46" :answer-field/id #uuid "9a59f474-95b3-51b1-a3ee-0b7d7cc3bba4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/choices ["q-110569/ma-source-field/4ad9718c1de7d4799fb874170518641b6a8b41e3a629d747aaebbf6123a3ea46/3074d4a4107f39cd5bb116e03eda439c83486015a0d56a6067142a21314822c3" "q-110569/ma-source-field/4ad9718c1de7d4799fb874170518641b6a8b41e3a629d747aaebbf6123a3ea46/c286394386ae9fb6724e458b654d34895a2465038e1d8d690ba0010ee69c36e5" "q-110569/ma-source-field/4ad9718c1de7d4799fb874170518641b6a8b41e3a629d747aaebbf6123a3ea46/f63d9b50facb458b4c375a9c5e3d85c45a34fbcc8e9afe9c2dd2b9d30c432dea" "q-110569/ma-source-field/4ad9718c1de7d4799fb874170518641b6a8b41e3a629d747aaebbf6123a3ea46/159bc988b0cb6f37bb060d75df30ae131cd06ae0bb0909a42adf041e1320835b" "q-110569/ma-source-field/4ad9718c1de7d4799fb874170518641b6a8b41e3a629d747aaebbf6123a3ea46/03fa27ae505cfc4e871c84f9928a5984fe4ef4a8b8755ec19a84ca4b37642294"] :answer-field/correct "q-110569/ma-source-field/4ad9718c1de7d4799fb874170518641b6a8b41e3a629d747aaebbf6123a3ea46/159bc988b0cb6f37bb060d75df30ae131cd06ae0bb0909a42adf041e1320835b" :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]}
 {:db/id "q-110569" :question/id #uuid "fcd0c175-5061-5e65-ae35-dc31f2b4c302" :question/math-academy-id "q-110569" :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 3 & -1 & 2\\end{aligned}],\\,J=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices above. Given that $A=PJ{P}^{-1}$ is a Jordan canonical decomposition of $A,$ which of the following could be the second column of the matrix $P?$\n\n$$\nx=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,y=[\\begin{aligned}7 \\\\ 13 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,z=[\\begin{aligned}-12 \\\\ -12 \\\\ 8\\end{aligned}]\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that the Jordan matrix $J$ may be written as a composition of Jordan blocks, as follows:\n\n$$\nJ={J}_{2}(-1)\\,⊕\\,{J}_{1}(2)\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n- one $2\\times 2$ Jordan block corresponding to $\\lambda =-1,$ and\n\n- one $1\\times 1$ Jordan block corresponding to $\\lambda =2.$\n\nTherefore, the columns of $P$ form the following chains: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{J}_{2}(-1) & : & {v}_{2}\\overset{A+I\\,}{\\to }{v}_{1}\\overset{A+I\\,}{\\to }0 &  \\\\ {J}_{1}(2) & : & {v}_{3}\\overset{A-2I\\,}{\\to }0 & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, the second column of $P$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $2$ corresponding to $\\lambda =-1.$ \n\nNow, computing $A+I,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A+I & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 3 & -1 & 2\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 3 & -1 & 3\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAmong the given vectors, only $y$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $2,$ since\n\n$$\n(A+I)y=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 3 & -1 & 3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}7 \\\\ 13 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-12 \\\\ -12 \\\\ 8\\end{aligned}]=z,\n$$\n\nand, therefore,\n\n$$\n(A+I)^{2}y=(A+I)z=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 3 & -1 & 3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-12 \\\\ -12 \\\\ 8\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nand we can write\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}7 \\\\ 13 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\overset{A+I\\,}{\\to }[\\begin{aligned}-12 \\\\ -12 \\\\ 8\\end{aligned}]\\overset{A+I\\,}{\\to }0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe other vectors do not satisfy this condition." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-110569/ma-source-field/4ad9718c1de7d4799fb874170518641b6a8b41e3a629d747aaebbf6123a3ea46"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]}
