 {:db/id "q-110754/ma-source-field/3f05140399a769a05cecf539e4873e48460513e1d37671e0efd1f3af09ca36aa" :answer-field/id #uuid "2c3251d1-6964-5600-84fe-fbb97c423ff0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/choices ["q-110754/ma-source-field/3f05140399a769a05cecf539e4873e48460513e1d37671e0efd1f3af09ca36aa/4eee2ad9db4dc998d20b3a2c4b42800bcafa83eefc60d2242c36a02b20f2d191" "q-110754/ma-source-field/3f05140399a769a05cecf539e4873e48460513e1d37671e0efd1f3af09ca36aa/e6a7043e863a085f9dae1f84128328c6663027614997fba954494180a9a2fae8" "q-110754/ma-source-field/3f05140399a769a05cecf539e4873e48460513e1d37671e0efd1f3af09ca36aa/8784b668a6ba0eba457705a4866827af77950ff4ee45c126138e84c967458084" "q-110754/ma-source-field/3f05140399a769a05cecf539e4873e48460513e1d37671e0efd1f3af09ca36aa/a2e88d846d6b0c42677d6f9397d2e766eeb74769cb264785021b19d45a13a124" "q-110754/ma-source-field/3f05140399a769a05cecf539e4873e48460513e1d37671e0efd1f3af09ca36aa/0e049999fb122e0194538e9a565c9b0f684a87c7ad9e3d499f6a3ab6c33820d5"] :answer-field/correct "q-110754/ma-source-field/3f05140399a769a05cecf539e4873e48460513e1d37671e0efd1f3af09ca36aa/8784b668a6ba0eba457705a4866827af77950ff4ee45c126138e84c967458084" :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]}
 {:db/id "q-110754" :question/id #uuid "b32c3e49-95fd-5233-9aa7-ff2e20c39b2e" :question/math-academy-id "q-110754" :question/problem "Consider the vectors ${a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}],$ and ${a}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}].$ Find a basis of $S=\\text{Span}{{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}}$  by inspection." :question/worked-solution "Remember that a basis of $S$ is a set of linearly independent vectors that spans $S.$\n\nFrom the given vectors, we can see that ${a}_{3}$ is a linear combination of vectors ${a}_{1}$ and ${a}_{2}:$\n\n$$\n{a}_{3}=1\\cdot {a}_{1}+1\\cdot {a}_{2}\n$$\n\nConsequently, the vector ${a}_{3}$ can be removed from $\\text{Span}{{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}}$ without changing the span. So $S=\\text{Span}{{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}}$ is spanned by just ${{a}_{1},{a}_{2}}.$ \n\nLastly, note that ${a}_{1}$ and ${a}_{2}$ are linearly independent since they are not parallel. So the set ${{a}_{1},{a}_{2}}$ is a linearly independent set that spans $S.$\n\nTherefore, a basis of $S$ is ${{a}_{1},{a}_{2}},$ that is to say,\n\n$$\nB={{{[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{a}_{1}},{{[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{a}_{2}}}.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-110754/ma-source-field/3f05140399a769a05cecf539e4873e48460513e1d37671e0efd1f3af09ca36aa"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]}
