 {:db/id "q-110756" :question/id #uuid "4400c4d4-e4b9-52ce-9f78-148918755004" :question/math-academy-id "q-110756" :question/problem "Consider the vectors ${a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}],{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],$ and ${a}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}].$ Find a basis of $S=\\text{Span}{{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}}$  by inspection." :question/worked-solution "Remember that a basis of $S$ is a set of linearly independent vectors that spans $S.$\n\nFrom the given vectors, we can see that ${a}_{3}$ is a linear combination of vectors ${a}_{1}$ and ${a}_{2}:$\n\n$$\n{a}_{3}=1\\cdot {a}_{1}+1\\cdot {a}_{2}\n$$\n\nConsequently, the vector ${a}_{3}$ can be removed from $\\text{Span}{{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}}$ without changing the span. So $S=\\text{Span}{{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}}$ is spanned by just ${{a}_{1},{a}_{2}}.$ \n\nLastly, note that ${a}_{1}$ and ${a}_{2}$ are linearly independent since they are not parallel. So the set ${{a}_{1},{a}_{2}}$ is a linearly independent set that spans $S.$\n\nTherefore, a basis of $S$ is ${{a}_{1},{a}_{2}},$ that is to say,\n\n$$\nB={{{[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{a}_{1}},{{[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{a}_{2}}}.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-110756/ma-source-field/5539324edc527686bd77f99d37190e64c485af779b33e5d12cf0f7d9efe47c6d"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]}
