 {:db/id [:question/id #uuid "7e0868ec-2423-5a95-9212-2ae581f51f78"] :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,J=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices above. Given that $A=PJ{P}^{-1}$ is a Jordan canonical decomposition of $A,$ which of the following could be the first column of the matrix $P?$\n\n$$\nx=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,y=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,z=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that the Jordan matrix $J$ consists of one Jordan block $J={J}_{2}(0).$ \n\nTherefore, the columns of $P$ form the following chain: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{J}_{2}(0) & : & {v}_{2}\\overset{A-0I\\,}{\\to }{v}_{1}\\overset{A-0I\\,}{\\to }0 & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, the first column of $P$ is an eigenvector corresponding to $\\lambda =0.$ \n\nNow, computing $A-0I,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-0I & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}]-0[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAmong the given vectors, only $x$ is an eigenvector of $A,$ since\n\n$$\n(A-0I)x=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nand we can write\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\overset{A-0I\\,}{\\to }0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe other vectors do not satisfy this condition." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-112432/ma-source-field/ca364621ed7fbce7c25aee2964aaa073cd16ff8e88c2f7cbfed27527252bc0fd"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]}
