 {:db/id "q-112500" :question/id #uuid "f6742def-6999-5043-9553-f817f1ed7b14" :question/math-academy-id "q-112500" :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}-6 & -8 \\\\ 2 & 2\\end{aligned}],\\,J=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices above. Given that $A=PJ{P}^{-1}$ is a Jordan canonical decomposition of $A,$ which of the following could be the second column of the matrix $P?$\n\n$$\nx=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}],\\,y=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}],\\,z=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that the Jordan matrix $J$ consists of one Jordan block $J={J}_{2}(-2).$  \n\nTherefore, the columns of $P$ form the following chain: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{J}_{2}(-2) & : & {v}_{2}\\overset{A-(-2)I\\,}{\\to }{v}_{1}\\overset{A-(-2)I\\,}{\\to }0 & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, the second column of $P$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $2$ corresponding to $\\lambda =-2.$ \n\nNow, computing $A-(-2)I,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-(-2)I & =[\\begin{aligned}-6 & -8 \\\\ 2 & 2\\end{aligned}]-(-2)[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-4 & -8 \\\\ 2 & 4\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nAmong the given vectors, only $x$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $2,$ since\n\n$$\n(A-(-2)I)x=[\\begin{aligned}-4 & -8 \\\\ 2 & 4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]=y,\n$$\n\nand, therefore,\n\n$$\n(A-(-2)I)^{2}x=(A+2I)y=[\\begin{aligned}-4 & -8 \\\\ 2 & 4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nand we can write\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]\\overset{A-(-2)I\\,}{\\to }[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]\\overset{A-(-2)I\\,}{\\to }0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThe other vectors do not satisfy this condition." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/a