 {:db/id "q-112863" :question/id #uuid "8da2d1e9-08f2-56a5-bfb7-528f2e2d7e75" :question/math-academy-id "q-112863" :question/problem "According to the rational roots theorem, which of the following could be a root of the polynomial $f(x)=7{x}^{3}-3{x}^{2}+x-14?$" :question/worked-solution "Given a polynomial with integer coefficients, the rational roots theorem states that any rational roots of the polynomial must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{\\text{factor of the constant term}}{\\text{factor of the leading coefficient}}.\n$$\n\nFor the given polynomial $f(x),$ the constant term is $-14,$ and the leading coefficient is $7.$ So, any rational roots of $f(x)$ must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{\\text{factor of }-14}{\\text{factor of }7}.\n$$\n\nOf the given answer choices, only $-2=\\frac{-2}{1}$ takes the above form since the numerator $(-2)$ is a factor of $-14$ and the denominator $(1)$ is a factor of $7.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-112863/ma-source-field/4f4f7a2759bbc51fc87c227d5ccfd1259f37120e33aa3e20d756e0c95c626bef"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]}
