 {:db/id "q-113554" :question/id #uuid "e0085fda-c140-5022-a903-4aa2d0060987" :question/math-academy-id "q-113554" :question/problem "Determine the vertical asymptotes of $f(x)=\\frac{2{x}^{2}-6}{{x}^{4}-9}.$" :question/worked-solution "To find the vertical asymptotes of a rational function, we factor the numerator and denominator and cancel any common factors. Then, we set the denominator equal to zero and solve for $x.$\n\nFactoring the numerator and denominator and canceling any common factors, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{2{x}^{2}-6}{{x}^{4}-9} \\\\  & =\\frac{2({x}^{2}-3)}{({x}^{2}-3)({x}^{2}+3)} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\cancel{({x}^{2}-3)}}{\\cancel{({x}^{2}-3)}({x}^{2}+3)} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}+3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we set the denominator equal to zero:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+3=0\n$$\n\nThis equation does not have any real solutions. Therefore, $f(x)$ has no vertical asymptotes." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["q-113554/ma-source-field/7c90d84e8a42ac38799a1b95aa8b33bd4d532bed7c67507a893dcbaab4c18e7a"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]}
