 {:db/id "q-113767/ma-source-field/7b23162d0b18c9b97e317dc5c99bac49534091d6f631ec3f5dd080f915df77ef" :answer-field/id #uuid "9f06b88a-0b3e-517c-8884-684e65abf6f2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/choices ["q-113767/ma-source-field/7b23162d0b18c9b97e317dc5c99bac49534091d6f631ec3f5dd080f915df77ef/c112c665879534c7ad1d1042a8b28b7e5f3f70117c93b42716ffdd8ce86d808a" "q-113767/ma-source-field/7b23162d0b18c9b97e317dc5c99bac49534091d6f631ec3f5dd080f915df77ef/d9b70cb012b8a98348e0eb2b076c0ffaa03d2f9b5ed0b778c333cec60818df33" "q-113767/ma-source-field/7b23162d0b18c9b97e317dc5c99bac49534091d6f631ec3f5dd080f915df77ef/f6a9948068396877505e54f66bfd517f08252fd5fcfe067bfbdff6bc25b00dda" "q-113767/ma-source-field/7b23162d0b18c9b97e317dc5c99bac49534091d6f631ec3f5dd080f915df77ef/8868f02215a5337e2a5129e75503fbec783bb64f355b374ffdd731bd6e356bdb" "q-113767/ma-source-field/7b23162d0b18c9b97e317dc5c99bac49534091d6f631ec3f5dd080f915df77ef/acc83c74f03748b2e35d16f5b1d34df696d35c1cc9214487ece6a3d7d0147310"] :answer-field/correct "q-113767/ma-source-field/7b23162d0b18c9b97e317dc5c99bac49534091d6f631ec3f5dd080f915df77ef/c112c665879534c7ad1d1042a8b28b7e5f3f70117c93b42716ffdd8ce86d808a" :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]}
 {:db/id "q-113767" :question/id #uuid "5d605cec-3072-5304-adcf-70f0f4b40d1d" :question/math-academy-id "q-113767" :question/problem "Given the function $f(x)=\\frac{{x}^{2}+1}{{e}^{x}},$ which of the following statements are true?" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the stationary points. Differentiating $f(x)$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{2x{e}^{x}-({x}^{2}+1){e}^{x}}{{e}^{2x}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{{x}^{2}-2x+1}{{e}^{x}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{(x-1)^{2}}{{e}^{x}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving ${f}^{′}(x)=0$ gives\n\n$$\n-\\frac{(x-1)^{2}}{{e}^{x}}\\,⟹\\,x=1.\n$$\n\nNote that we used the fact that ${e}^{x}>0$ for all values of $x.$\n\nSo, $x=1$ is the only stationary point of $f(x).$ Next, to determine whether this point is a local maximum or minimum, we use the first derivative test.\n\nLet's choose the test points $x=0,2$ and evaluate the first derivative at these points.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(0) & =-\\frac{(0-1)^{2}}{{e}^{0}}=-1<0 \\\\ {f}^{′}(2) & =-\\frac{(2-1)^{2}}{{e}^{2}}=-{e}^{-2}<0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe summarize the information in a table, shown below.\n\n| $x$ | $(-\\infty ,1)$ | $1$ | $(1,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Test Point | $0$ |  | $2$ |\n| ${f}^{′}$ | $-$ | $0$ | $-$ |\n| $f$ | $↘$ |  | $↘$ |\n\nThe first derivative does not change sign at $x=1.$ Therefore, we conclude that $x=1$ is neither a minimum nor a maximum.\n\nTherefore, the first derivative test shows that the function has no local extrema." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-113767/ma-source-field/7b23162d0b18c9b97e317dc5c99bac49534091d6f631ec3f5dd080f915df77ef"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]}
