 {:db/id "q-114399" :question/id #uuid "959a29f9-94e3-5ec3-bf75-8100c4fac3c4" :question/math-academy-id "q-114399" :question/problem "![](images/8f/8f20b09e85ce71a1001e7c701ebfafa3e6ac6c8adb29e8dfd47f256f37a29f5f.png)\n\nThe function $h(x)$ is continuous and twice-differentiable for all $x$ in $(-4,1.5)$ The graph $y={h}^{″}(x)$ is shown above. At what value(s) of $x$ does $h(x)$ have a point of inflection?" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that since $h(x)$ is twice-differentiable, the tangent line to the graph exists for all real $x.$\n\nInflection points occur when a graph changes concavity. In order for the graph to change concavity, the value of the second derivative ${h}^{″}(x)$ must switch signs. So, we are looking for points where the graph of ${h}^{″}(x)$ crosses the $x$-axis.\n\nWe can see that the graph $y={h}^{″}(x)$ crosses the $x$-axis at $x=-1$ and $x=1.$ \n\nTherefore, $h(x)$ has points of inflection at $x=-1$ and $x=1.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-114399/ma-source-field/2522407965a670789f45ceff7d20a1810a17e0c2c4b219d84493104650060228"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]}
