 {:db/id "q-114402" :question/id #uuid "444031a3-3309-59e7-a714-c81365022d27" :question/math-academy-id "q-114402" :question/problem "![](images/20/20b6532fc5d99c74a26bd752e19aa2a6451ea26a79d2a129e4c0203bc9133354.png)\n\nThe function $f(x)$ is continuous and twice-differentiable for all $x$ in $(-4,4).$ The graph $y={f}^{″}(x)$ is shown above. At what value(s) of $x$ does $f(x)$ have a point of inflection?" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that since $f(x)$ is twice-differentiable, the tangent line to the graph exists for all real $x.$\n\nInflection points occur when a graph changes concavity. In order for the graph to change concavity, the value of the second derivative ${f}^{″}(x)$ must switch signs. So, we are looking for points where the graph of ${f}^{″}(x)$ crosses the $x$-axis.\n\nWe can see that the graph $y={f}^{″}(x)$ crosses the $x$-axis at $x=-2,$ $x=1,$ and $x=3.$ \n\nTherefore, $f(x)$ has points of inflection at $x=-2,$ $x=1,$ and $x=3.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-114402/ma-source-field/273e037d2bb0344f05cd677595190c92a281032b59cdf4acd9a66b476ad9a16c"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]}
