 {:db/id "q-115311/ma-source-field/44fb6e3818bf16d18d34c059cc5305f59fa2bedcdc2ac8e098e897a2f0c3185d" :answer-field/id #uuid "1872eebc-f1ce-56c5-86dd-93ce7b60f7ed" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/choices ["q-115311/ma-source-field/44fb6e3818bf16d18d34c059cc5305f59fa2bedcdc2ac8e098e897a2f0c3185d/5b815160609f9a0c4cbc066ed960fd139af67bfcf8505cf642e2f2fe5dbb8231" "q-115311/ma-source-field/44fb6e3818bf16d18d34c059cc5305f59fa2bedcdc2ac8e098e897a2f0c3185d/18b7ef853c01b9c52196a4f7e75b76bd80bcc19d3599e5cfab8466feb9b00176" "q-115311/ma-source-field/44fb6e3818bf16d18d34c059cc5305f59fa2bedcdc2ac8e098e897a2f0c3185d/c9bae6c1aa3f5bebaa81e0dcaef93adfa7f7c1ad92f6a914dcf89a0669ee99f4" "q-115311/ma-source-field/44fb6e3818bf16d18d34c059cc5305f59fa2bedcdc2ac8e098e897a2f0c3185d/c9057d118480f81bdbdf1727a5b60ebe7d6873887f1f97aee05638dde2139103" "q-115311/ma-source-field/44fb6e3818bf16d18d34c059cc5305f59fa2bedcdc2ac8e098e897a2f0c3185d/11b5fc3fa0de03291a8c9b3264577bcd8265fdac26fd775b7f00b2640e7f9b5a"] :answer-field/correct "q-115311/ma-source-field/44fb6e3818bf16d18d34c059cc5305f59fa2bedcdc2ac8e098e897a2f0c3185d/c9bae6c1aa3f5bebaa81e0dcaef93adfa7f7c1ad92f6a914dcf89a0669ee99f4" :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]}
 {:db/id "q-115311" :question/id #uuid "023cf167-60aa-5f09-9ef1-d06a20d0ee94" :question/math-academy-id "q-115311" :question/problem "For the ellipse $\\frac{(x+6)^{2}}{5}+\\frac{(y+4)^{2}}{9}=1$, find the lengths of its major and minor axes." :question/worked-solution "Remember that the general equation of an ellipse centered at $(h,k)$ with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{(x-h)^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{(y-k)^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn the given equation, we have $h=-6$ and $k=-4,$ so the center is located at $(-6,-4).$\n\nIn the given equation, we also have ${a}^{2}=5$ and ${b}^{2}=9.$\n\n- Since ${a}^{2}=5,$ the length of the horizontal radius must be $a=\\sqrt{5}.$\n\n- Since ${b}^{2}=9,$ the length of the vertical radius must be $b=\\sqrt{9}=3.$\n\nSo the semi-major axis has length $b=3,$ and the semi-minor axis has length $a=\\sqrt{5}.$\n\nTherefore, the major axis has length $2b=2\\cdot 3=6,$ and the minor axis has length $2a=2\\cdot \\sqrt{5}=2\\sqrt{5}.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-115311/ma-source-field/44fb6e3818bf16d18d34c059cc5305f59fa2bedcdc2ac8e098e897a2f0c3185d"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]}
