 {:db/id "q-115479/ma-source-field/5802215430ff263e51701cadf242fe8cad4e988325695c09f078d4dc3ade3562" :answer-field/id #uuid "6e99c72e-4b83-5737-a710-a4529e2905bd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/choices ["q-115479/ma-source-field/5802215430ff263e51701cadf242fe8cad4e988325695c09f078d4dc3ade3562/431472f1dab61ef54de031c09a96fb6a21dc69c8bc09f12797d1d686e8c9e8e6" "q-115479/ma-source-field/5802215430ff263e51701cadf242fe8cad4e988325695c09f078d4dc3ade3562/5b26512916f768e66a2b0af106e5aa3e2ff7ee012277d9669a26d349c343f77b" "q-115479/ma-source-field/5802215430ff263e51701cadf242fe8cad4e988325695c09f078d4dc3ade3562/1f1f2fe9df6485d5cdc7916416b2f79699d874348406e639d5daec02cb2e57e6" "q-115479/ma-source-field/5802215430ff263e51701cadf242fe8cad4e988325695c09f078d4dc3ade3562/df67a18079e3b7b726c109ae1bf07ae161d9f6d37a928c50361c8787d74f7981" "q-115479/ma-source-field/5802215430ff263e51701cadf242fe8cad4e988325695c09f078d4dc3ade3562/6a87b4860f595a380edef797b2cc43b24d4896fc51da713259b980588721456c"] :answer-field/correct "q-115479/ma-source-field/5802215430ff263e51701cadf242fe8cad4e988325695c09f078d4dc3ade3562/6a87b4860f595a380edef797b2cc43b24d4896fc51da713259b980588721456c" :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]}
 {:db/id [:question/id #uuid "85b66c06-8b49-5d46-861f-2424f521305f"] :question/problem "![](images/1b/1b608fabe99c6062f9d9f04cc7f3b2933e453812ed4ce9783908666c71c1c74b.png)\n\nWhich of the following equations could have generated the slope field shown above?\n\nI. ${y}^{′}=\\frac{y}{y-1}$\nII. ${y}^{′}=\\frac{1}{y}$\nIII. ${y}^{′}=\\frac{1}{{y}^{2}}$" :question/worked-solution "![](images/7b/7bb52825944a7515a6281a88b5bc740cf6d097380fa13fe7e00efc5e499d49f8.png)\n\n \n\nNotice the following properties of the given slope field:\n\n- The slopes of the slope field become vertical as $y\\to 0.$\n\n- The slopes of the slope field become horizontal as $y\\to \\pm \\infty .$\n\n- The slopes of the slope field are positive everywhere.\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each equation.\n\n- The equation ${y}^{′}=\\frac{y}{y-1}$ does *not* give the required behavior. Notice that\n\n$$\n\\frac{y}{y-1}=\\frac{1}{(1-\\frac{1}{y})}\\to 1\\,\\text{as}\\,y\\to \\pm \\infty .\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slopes of the slope field for this differential equation do not become horizontal as $y\\to \\pm \\infty .$\n\n- The equation ${y}^{′}=\\frac{1}{y}$ does *not* give the required behavior. The slopes of the slope field for this differential equation are negative for $y<0.$\n\n- The equation ${y}^{′}=\\frac{1}{{y}^{2}}$ gives the required behavior. Notice that ${y}^{′}\\to 0$ as $y\\to \\infty ,$ and ${y}^{′}\\to \\infty $ as $y\\to 0.$ Also, the right-hand side of this equation is positive for all $y\\ne 0.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["q-115479/ma-source-field/5802215430ff263e51701cadf242fe8cad4e988325695c09f078d4dc3ade3562"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]}
